✅ Types of Computer Networks – 
🔹1. LAN – Local Area Network
  A Local Area Network (LAN) connects computers and devices within a limited area, like a home, school, or office.

🧠 Key Features:
High-speed (100 Mbps to 10+ Gbps)
Low latency and error rate
Privately managed
Ideal for internal file sharing, printing, and communication
 
⚙️ Technical Details:
Range: Up to 1 km
Speed: 100 Mbps – 10+ Gbps
Medium: Ethernet cables (Cat5e, Cat6), Wi-Fi
Protocols: Ethernet (IEEE 802.3), Wi-Fi (802.11), TCP/IP

✅ Use Cases:
Office and home networks
School computer labs
Small business setups

📌 Advantages:
Fast data transfer
Secure within local boundaries
Easy troubleshooting and maintenance

❌ Limitations:
Limited range (within a building or campus)
Physical cable setup (for Ethernet LAN)


🔹2. WLAN – Wireless Local Area Network
    A WLAN is a wireless variant of LAN where devices connect via radio waves instead of Ethernet cables.

🧠 Key Features:
No physical wiring
Mobility within range of signal
Operates in 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz frequency bands
Uses SSIDs and encryption (WPA2/WPA3) for security

⚙️ Technical Details:
Range: ~100 meters indoors1
Speed: 100 Mbps – 9.6 Gbps (Wi-Fi 6/6E)
Standards: IEEE 802.11a/b/g/n/ac/ax
Security: WPA3 (latest standard

✅ Use Cases:
Home Wi-Fi networks
Cafes, airports, malls
Enterprise wireless environments (BYOD)

📌 Advantages:
Easy to install and expand
Enables device mobility
No physical cable clutter

❌ Limitations:
Less secure (prone to attacks if open)
Speed and reliability affected by interference

🔹 3. PAN – Personal Area Network
📘 Definition:
A Personal Area Network (PAN) is a very small network used to connect personal devices over short distances (typically <10 meters).

🧠 Key Features:
Centered around a single user
Used for short-range, low-data transmission
Can be wired (USB) or wireless (Bluetooth, Infrared)

🛠 Technologies:
Bluetooth: For wireless device pairing
Zigbee: Used in IoT devices
Infrared: Older tech for line-of-sight connections

✅ Use Cases:
Smartwatch paired with smartphone
Bluetooth headset
File transfer between phone and laptop

📌 Advantages:
Ultra-low power consumption
Simple to set up
No external infrastructure needed

❌ Limitations:
Very limited range and speed

Not suited for data-intensive or multi-user applications

🔹 4. CAN – Campus Area Network
📘 Definition:
A Campus Area Network (CAN) connects multiple LANs across a campus-like environment — such as a university, military base, or large corporation.

🧠 Key Features:
Covers 1–10 km radius
speed: 1 Gbps %E2%80%93 100 Gbps
Managed by a single organization
Supports high-speed and high-volume data transfer
Usually has a fiber-optic backbone

🛠 Technologies:
Fiber Ethernet backbone
Routers and switches for inter-building LAN connection
VPN and firewall security solutions

✅ Use Cases:
College/university campuses (labs, hostels, libraries)
Corporate headquarters with multiple departments
Manufacturing plants or research facilities

📌 Advantages:
Centralized control
High-speed intra-campus communication
Scalable to connect thousands of devices

❌ Limitations:
High initial cost for installation
Requires skilled network management

🔹 5. MAN – Metropolitan Area Network
  A Metropolitan Area Network (MAN) spans an entire city or large urban area, interconnecting multiple LANs and CANs.

🧠 Key Features:
Covers up to 50 km
May be publicly or privately owned
Uses high-speed data lines (fiber optic)

Example:
Ek city ke andar alag-alag college campuses ko connect karna.
Cable TV network.
Bank ki city ke andar different branches ko connect karna.

⚙️ Technical Details:
Range: Up to ~50 km
Speed: 10 Mbps – 1 Gbps
Technology: Metro Ethernet, FDDI, DSL
Provider: Usually operated by ISPs or government

✅ Use Cases:
City-wide government networks
University branches across a cit
Corporate buildings in multiple city locations

📌 Advantages:
Facilitates centralized data centers
High-speed communication over wide urban areas

❌ Limitations:
Expensive to build and maintain
Performance varies with distance and load

🔹 6. WAN – Wide Area Network
A Wide Area Network (WAN) spans large geographic areas, connecting networks across countries or continents.

🧠 Key Features:Features:
Long distance communication provide karta hai.
Multiple LANs aur MANs ko connect karta hai.
Data transmission ke liye public ya private communication lines use karta hai.
Setup aur maintenance cost zyada hoti hai.
Large-scale organizations aur Internet me use hota hai.

Example:
Internet sabse bada WAN hai.
Ek company ke Delhi, Mumbai aur Chennai offices ko connect karna WAN ka example hai.

⚙️ Technical Details:
Range: Unlimited (global)
Speed: Varies – from Kbps to 100+ Gbps
Mediums: Leased lines, fiber optics, satellite, undersea cables
Protocols: MPLS, IPsec VPN, BGP, OSPF

✅ Use Cases:
The Internet
Banking networks (ATM connectivity)
Global enterprise communications
Cloud services and SaaS platforms

📌 Advantages:
Enables global connectivity and communication
Centralized access to remote resources

❌ Limitations:
High setup and operational cost
Prone to latency, packet loss, and attacks

